diff --git a/docs/beetle-oracle.md b/docs/beetle-oracle.md
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/beetle-oracle.md
@@ -0,0 +1,89 @@
+# Restoring the independent Beetle oracle
+
+The compatible upstream base is
+`5759277be50052b9f3f388578bf56cc7899d833f` from
+<https://github.com/libretro/beetle-psx-libretro>.
+`beetle_oracle_hooks.patch` is a consolidated patch against that exact base;
+it replaces the three older partial patches. Do not apply both sets.
+The patch modifies the GPL-2.0-or-later Beetle core and retains its license.
+The checkout and compiled libraries stay in gitignored `beetle-psx/`.
+
+On Windows, with MSYS2 MinGW64 GCC, make, CMake, Ninja, SDL2 and zlib installed:
+
+```powershell
+./tools/build_beetle_oracle.ps1
+```
+
+This builds `runtime/build-oracle/psx-beetle.exe` with RelWithDebInfo and TCP
+debugging. The script preserves an existing checkout and fails on a pin or
+patch conflict. SDL2 is explicitly selected for this oracle harness. The
+game project can independently select SDL3. No generated BIOS or game code
+is needed to build this independent executable. `ALLOW_NO_BIOS` is used
+only to configure the shared CMake project; the native runtime is not built
+or verified by this command.
+
+For local launch, put `C:/msys64/mingw64/bin` on PATH for its DLLs. Use a
+gitignored build directory as the working directory so oracle memory cards
+and captures stay local:
+
+```powershell
+$env:PATH = "C:\msys64\mingw64\bin;$env:PATH"
+# Run from runtime/build-oracle/verification (create it first).
+../psx-beetle.exe ../../../bios/openbios.bin --disc C:/dumps/game.cue --port 4380 --wtrace-boot
+```
+
+The harness selects upstream's `openbios` firmware option when the provided
+filename is `openbios.bin` (also the upstream uppercase spellings). Always
+verify the actual ROM through `read_ram`; accepting a BIOS path alone is not
+proof of which firmware libretro loaded.
+
+The consolidated hooks expose CPU data reads/writes, J/JAL/JR/JALR calls,
+completed SIO bytes, CD command dispatch, IRQ rising edges, SPU key/block
+events, the live CPU cycle clock, and exception entries. They observe existing
+core operations without altering instructions, device state, or timing.
+Cycle-watch uses instruction-entry timestamps, including cold instruction
+fetch costs in an A-to-B delta. Device-event cycles stamp the CPU service
+time; they do not claim a separate peripheral edge timestamp.
+
+`beetle_observe.cpp` implements bounded cycle-watch and exception rings;
+these commands previously returned placeholders. The core and frontend both
+define `__LIBRETRO__` to keep the C++ CDC layout identical. Call tracing
+uses the current instruction PC rather than the frame-end backing PC.
+
+For a running program, choose two observed PCs on a recurring path and run:
+
+```powershell
+python tools/verify_beetle_oracle.py --bios bios/openbios.bin `
+  --anchor 0x00001C04 --end 0x00001C08 `
+  --out runtime/build-oracle/verification/oracle-proof.json
+```
+
+Those example PCs are in the pinned OpenBIOS timer callback; they were
+observed while Final Fantasy Tactics (USA) was running. This smoke test
+requires active disc, pad, SPU, and interrupt traffic. A program that does not
+exercise those paths will fail the relevant checks. Inspect the emitted PNG
+as the separate visual gate. The JSON, PNG and WAV remain local build output.
+
+## Software raster comparisons
+
+`psx-beetle --headless` retains the core, TCP server and frame pacing while
+omitting the oracle window and audio device. This permits a single visible game
+window during native/oracle comparisons.
+
+The TCP `gpu_probe` command accepts a `words` string of concatenated eight-digit
+hex GP0 words. It saves the core, creates a procedural 15-bit texture, submits
+the packet, returns a 128 × 128 raw pixel rectangle, and restores the core. A
+failed restoration aborts instead of continuing with modified game state. This
+is an intentional diagnostic GPU operation, separate from passive event rings.
+
+Build the `gpu_sw_probe` runtime CMake target and run:
+
+```powershell
+python tools/verify_gpu_raster.py runtime/build-oracle/libgpu_sw_probe.dll `
+  --port 4380 --output runtime/build-oracle/verification/raster.json
+ctest --test-dir runtime/build-oracle -R gpu_sw_beetle_raster_test --output-on-failure
+```
+
+The live comparison checks 116 procedural raw-texture triangle cases. CTest
+checks the same native renderer against recorded Beetle hashes without requiring
+a live game or disc. The fixture includes no copyrighted game pixels or code.
